---
---
<div class="flex items-center justify-between pb-4 border-b border-gray-200">
<!-- Trigger Button -->
<button
        id="open-ats-modal"
        class="bg-slate-900 text-white px-4 py-2 rounded font-semibold hover:bg-slate-800 transition"
>
    The Harsh Reality of ATS
</button>

<!-- Native Dialog Element -->
<dialog
        id="ats-modal"
        class="backdrop:bg-black/60 p-0 rounded-lg shadow-2xl max-w-3xl w-[90vw] m-auto overflow-hidden text-gray-900"
>
    <div class="p-6 md:p-8 max-h-[85vh] overflow-y-auto">
        <div class="flex justify-between items-start border-b-2 border-gray-200 pb-4 mb-6">
            <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">The Harsh Reality of ATS Parsing</h2>
            <button
                    id="close-ats-modal"
                    class="text-gray-500 hover:text-gray-900 font-bold text-xl leading-none"
                    aria-label="Close"
            >
                &times;
            </button>
        </div>

        <ul class="space-y-5 text-base">
            <li>
                <strong class="font-bold">Visuals are Invisible:</strong> ATS parsers do not care about your design. Complex formatting like tables, text boxes, columns, and graphics will actively disrupt the scan and scramble your text into unreadable gibberish.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong class="font-bold">The "Auto-Reject" could be a Myth:</strong> Some ATS platforms might not auto-reject resumes simply for missing keywords. You might be failing the human review.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong class="font-bold">The "White Font" Trick is a Trap:</strong> Hiding keywords in white text to trick the parser will backfire. The ATS extracts everything into plain text, making you look desperate and dishonest to the recruiter.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong class="font-bold">Dates Require Strict Consistency:</strong> Mixing formats like "March 2023" and "03/2023" confuses the parser and can invent false employment gaps.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong class="font-bold">Creative Headers Kill Context:</strong> Labeling your experience section "My Journey" means the ATS will likely skip it entirely. Stick to standard headers like "Work Experience" and "Skills".
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong class="font-bold">Keyword Stuffing Fails Modern Parsers:</strong> Modern ATS software uses natural language processing. A bullet point that reads "Led a team of 5 to improve performance by 30%" beats a standalone list of keywords every time.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong class="font-bold">Headers and Footers are Dead Zones:</strong> Do not put your contact information in the document header or footer. Many parsers completely ignore these fields.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</dialog>
</div>

<script>
    const modal = document.getElementById('ats-modal') as HTMLDialogElement;
    const openBtn = document.getElementById('open-ats-modal');
    const closeBtn = document.getElementById('close-ats-modal');

    // Open modal
    openBtn?.addEventListener('click', () => {
        modal?.showModal();
    });

    // Close modal on button click
    closeBtn?.addEventListener('click', () => {
        modal?.close();
    });

    // Close modal when clicking on the backdrop
    modal?.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
        const dialogDimensions = modal.getBoundingClientRect();
        if (
            e.clientX < dialogDimensions.left ||
            e.clientX > dialogDimensions.right ||
            e.clientY < dialogDimensions.top ||
            e.clientY > dialogDimensions.bottom
        ) {
            modal.close();
        }
    });
</script>